4175	PERF-SQL-LC-005-RC01	20	1	Expensive queries within transaction	Queries with high execution time (complex joins, large scans, sorting) hold locks for extended periods.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	{"mysql": "No built-in transaction timeout (relies on application timeout). `max_execution_time` hint controls query timeout. InnoDB undo log for long-running transactions can prevent purge of deleted rows, slowing down other transactions. No automatic transaction timeout for idle connections (must be handled by application pooling layer).", "oracle": "`max_idle_blocker_time` parameter can kill idle blocking sessions. Long-running transactions prevent undo segment reuse, eventually causing \\"ORA-01555: snapshot too old\\" errors. UNDO_RETENTION parameter extends undo retention. Flashback can help recover from accidental long-transaction isolation issues. `DB_TIME` metric tracks actual transaction time in AWR reports.", "mariadb": "No built-in transaction timeout (relies on application timeout). `max_execution_time` hint controls query timeout. InnoDB undo log for long-running transactions can prevent purge of deleted rows, slowing down other transactions. No automatic transaction timeout for idle connections (must be handled by application pooling layer).", "sqlserver": "No transaction timeout built-in (must implement at application level). SNAPSHOT isolation allows reads but not writes during long transactions. Resource semaphores prevent memory pressure from long-running queries. Tempdb allocation tracking can reveal extent of undo needed for rolled-back long transactions.", "postgresql": "`statement_timeout` kills individual statements. `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` kills idle open transactions (very helpful). Default `deadlock_timeout` of 1 second will also log long lock waits. Vacuum must occur before XID wraparound (200 million transactions), forcing aggressive autovacuum. `pg_stat_activity` shows `xact_start` vs `query_start` to identify transaction duration."}	\N	\N
