5949	SEC-SQL-INJ-001-RC15	142	15	Incomplete parameter binding	Only some parameters are parameterized while others are concatenated; identifiers (table/column names) are concatenated because they cannot be parameterized, creating partial vulnerability. **Developer/Code Root Causes:** - **Rapid prototyping mentality** - Build functionality quickly first, refactor later; dynamic SQL is faster to write initially but riskier long-term. - **Knowledge gaps in secure patterns** - Developers trained on how to use stored procedures but not how to implement them securely with dynamic SQL. - **Misaligned incentives** - Success metrics focus on features delivered, not security vulnerabilities prevented; security is treated as an afterthought. - **Copy-paste programming** - Legacy code examples, online tutorials, and internal code repositories contain insecure dynamic SQL patterns that developers replicate. - **Underestimation of attack scenarios** - Belief that database security is "someone else's problem" or that firewalls/network controls are sufficient protection.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
5951	SEC-SQL-INJ-002-RC02	143	2	Backward compatibility requirements	Multi-customer or long-running systems cannot easily drop support for xp_cmdshell or other extended procedures without breaking dependent code; maintaining legacy interfaces takes priority.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
5954	SEC-SQL-INJ-002-RC05	143	5	High barrier to refactoring	Migrating from xp_cmdshell to alternatives requires database code changes, application code changes, infrastructure changes, and comprehensive testing; cost-benefit analysis often favors maintaining the status quo.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
5955	SEC-SQL-INJ-002-RC06	143	6	Operational automation dependencies	Database maintenance tasks (backup management, file system checks, log rotation, folder permissions) appear to require direct OS access; xp_cmdshell is the established solution in place.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
5956	SEC-SQL-INJ-002-RC07	143	7	Insufficient privilege controls	Developers grant sysadmin roles too broadly or fail to restrict who can enable/execute xp_cmdshell; the feature becomes accessible to users who don't truly need it.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
5957	SEC-SQL-INJ-002-RC08	143	8	Missing monitoring and enforcement	No detection mechanisms, audit policies, or triggers prevent unauthorized use or re-enabling of xp_cmdshell after it's been disabled; administrators can circumvent controls.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
5958	SEC-SQL-INJ-002-RC09	143	9	Assumption of network isolation	False belief that database servers are sufficiently isolated from threats; assumption that only trusted internal users can execute SQL injection attacks against database.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
5959	SEC-SQL-INJ-002-RC10	143	10	Security compliance misalignment	Compliance policies may require disabling xp_cmdshell, but the control itself is ineffective because sysadmins with CONTROL SERVER permissions can re-enable it at any time without restriction.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
5961	SEC-SQL-INJ-002-RC12	143	12	Third-party application requirements	Vendor software or legacy applications explicitly require xp_cmdshell; replacing or upgrading these systems is not feasible due to cost, compatibility, or business continuity concerns.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
5947	SEC-SQL-INJ-001-RC13	142	13	Operator control issues	Database operators or administrators use OS command execution features in maintenance jobs because built-in administrative tasks do not fully meet operational requirements.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
5950	SEC-SQL-INJ-002-RC01	143	1	Legacy system constraints	Extended stored procedures (xp_* functions) were the primary mechanism for OS-level operations in older SQL Server versions; replacing them requires significant refactoring of existing applications.	\N	{sqlserver}	\N	\N	\N
5952	SEC-SQL-INJ-002-RC03	143	3	Microsoft deprecation lag	Microsoft marked extended stored procedures as deprecated in SQL Server 2005+ but did not remove them until later versions, creating a false sense of safety for prolonged use.	\N	{sqlserver}	\N	\N	\N
5960	SEC-SQL-INJ-002-RC11	143	11	Service account privilege excess	SQL Server service accounts run with overly broad permissions (local admin, domain user rights); xp_cmdshell abuse leverages these excessive permissions.	\N	{sqlserver}	\N	\N	\N
5965	SEC-SQL-INJ-002-RC16	143	16	Insufficient removal validation	When xp_cmdshell is removed, hidden dependencies may exist in SQL Agent jobs, maintenance plans, or third-party tools; removal breaks functionality silently or causes operational failures. **Developer/Code Root Causes:** - **One-size-fits-all approach** - Using xp_cmdshell as the default for any OS-level requirement rather than evaluating purpose-built alternatives. - **Minimal security training** - Database developers and DBAs may lack understanding of privilege escalation, lateral movement, and how xp_cmdshell enables persistent access. - **Vendor lock-in acceptance** - Acceptance that certain vendor applications require xp_cmdshell; treating the requirement as non-negotiable rather than pushing back or seeking alternatives. - **Operational convenience over security** - Prioritizing ease of implementation and operational simplicity over security hardening; xp_cmdshell is the simplest known solution. - **Incident response gaps** - Lack of post-incident learning; compromises via xp_cmdshell may not be attributed to its use; lessons not incorporated into future development practices.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
5966	SEC-SQL-PAT-001-RC01	144	1	Lack of proactive EOL tracking and monitoring	Organizations fail to establish processes that monitor vendor EOL announcements, resulting in systems unknowingly drifting past support end dates without awareness or planning.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
5967	SEC-SQL-PAT-001-RC02	144	2	Deferred major version upgrades due to technical complexity	Major version upgrades require significant testing, potential data migration, application recompilation, or schema changes that demand substantial engineering effort, leading teams to postpone indefinitely.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
5970	SEC-SQL-PAT-001-RC05	144	5	Application dependency on deprecated database features	Older applications may rely on deprecated database functions, syntax, or behavior no longer present in newer versions, requiring significant application refactoring or rewriting before migration is feasible.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
5974	SEC-SQL-PAT-001-RC09	144	9	Client and driver compatibility concerns	Applications using database clients or drivers compiled against older versions may require relinking, recompilation, or updates that demand coordination across multiple teams and application owners.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
5976	SEC-SQL-PAT-001-RC11	144	11	Legacy application portfolio constraints	Some business-critical legacy applications were built on specific database versions and have not been maintained; attempting upgrades risks breaking these unsupported, unmaintained systems.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
5977	SEC-SQL-PAT-001-RC12	144	12	Incremental neglect over multiple release cycles	Organizations miss several consecutive EOL deadlines (e.g., "we'll upgrade from 11 to 12 eventually"), allowing the problem to compound until the cost becomes prohibitive.	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica}	\N	\N	\N
