6250	PERF-VEC-ING-002-RC01	482	1	HNSW Graph locking	Updating the Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) graph requires locking nodes to modify neighbors; high concurrency leads to thread contention on these "hot" entry points.	\N	{chromadb,milvus,qdrant,weaviate}	{"milvus": "The \\"Delegator\\" node effectively serializes write requests to log brokers. If the message queue (Pulsar/Kafka) is backlogged, the proxy nodes will block/wait.", "qdrant": "Uses a collection-level lock for some configuration updates, but data writes are generally async. However, heavy writes can saturate the WAL, causing backpressure on the gRPC endpoints.", "chromadb": "In local persistence mode, it is limited by SQLite's `SQLITE_BUSY` or `database is locked` errors if multiple processes/threads attempt to write simultaneously.", "weaviate": "Heavy write loads can trigger \\"Memtable flushes\\" which, if disk I/O is slow, will block new writes until memory is freed (backpressure).", "postgresql": "`HNSW` indexes use `HNSW_UPDATE_LOCK` (coarse) and page locks (fine). Heavy concurrent inserts on `IVFFlat` can degrade recall unless the index is rebuilt, whereas `HNSW` handles it but with higher locking cost."}	\N	\N
