6569	HLTH-VEC-MNT-001-RC18	505	18	Network bottlenecks for distributed shard movement	Compaction that involves shard relocation or merging across nodes is limited by network throughput, extending maintenance windows indefinitely. Vendor-Specific Notes: - Milvus: Compaction and WAL/binlog behavior governed by DataCoord and configuration; stale etcd state and binlog thresholds are common sources of stalls. - Qdrant: Background optimizer handles segment merging and vacuuming; its thresholds and segment-count settings must match ingest patterns. - Weaviate: Background maintenance over many shards/tenants can extend compaction times; scaling pattern and shard counts influence behavior. - pgvector/PostgreSQL: Autovacuum and REINDEX are the principal compaction-like tools; misconfigured autovacuum or low maintenance memory cause bloat. - MongoDB Atlas Vector Search: Compaction and cleanup are tied to replication health and cluster resource allocation. - Elasticsearch/OpenSearch: Force merge and ILM settings must be tuned to avoid long-running merges on large vector indices. - Pinecone, Chroma, Redis: Compaction is mostly automatic or implicit (e.g., RDB/AOF rewrite in Redis); issues arise when persistence or background maintenance is constrained or blocked.	\N	{chromadb,milvus,qdrant,weaviate}	\N	\N	\N
