4613	PERF-SQL-VS-001-RC14	50	14	Recovery/crash recovery running indefinitely	Incomplete crash recovery or replica recovery keeps minimal transaction IDs frozen, preventing normal VACUUM operations. **Architectural Context:** PostgreSQL uses a 32-bit unsigned integer for transaction IDs (XIDs). While this provides ~4 billion possible values, the MVCC visibility system divides the XID space into past and future: anything beyond 2^31 transactions from the current position wraps around. Rows store their creation (xmin) and deletion (xmax) XIDs; if wraparound occurs without freezing, old rows suddenly appear "in the future" and become invisible, corrupting data visibility. The VACUUM process freezes transaction IDs by replacing old xmin/xmax values with a special FrozenXID, making them visible regardless of wraparound. However, VACUUM cannot freeze rows modified by transactions that are still active or visible—hence it requires all old transaction IDs to be completed and their changes committed.	\N	{postgresql}	\N	\N	\N
4614	PERF-SQL-VS-002-RC01	51	1	Frequent UPDATE operations on TOAST-eligible columns	When TEXT, VARCHAR, BYTEA, or JSONB columns exceeding the 2 KB threshold are updated, PostgreSQL creates new versions in the TOAST table while marking old versions as dead, accumulating bloat.	\N	{postgresql}	\N	\N	\N
4623	PERF-SQL-VS-002-RC10	51	10	Partial compression due to pglz inefficiency	PostgreSQL's pglz compression (25-50% typical ratio) works well on text but poorly on sparse or structured data; inadequately-compressed values still consume TOAST storage.	\N	{postgresql}	\N	\N	\N
4628	PERF-SQL-VS-002-RC15	51	15	System catalog TOAST bloat (pg_description, pg_attribute comments)	Comments on columns and objects stored in TOAST catalog tables can accumulate dead versions if frequently modified, slowing catalog queries. **Architectural Context:** PostgreSQL's TOAST (The Oversized-Attribute Storage Technique) solves the problem of fitting large values into fixed 8 KB pages. When a tuple exceeds `TOAST_TUPLE_THRESHOLD` (default 2 KB), PostgreSQL attempts compression, then out-of-line storage in a separate TOAST table. TOAST tables are stored identically to regular tables—with MVCC, autovacuum, and the same bloat mechanics. Each column's TOAST strategy (EXTENDED, MAIN, EXTERNAL, PLAIN) controls whether compression and/or out-of-line storage is attempted. The TOAST mechanism is essential for correctness but creates a secondary storage space that bloats independently if autovacuum cannot keep pace with updates. Unlike table bloat (which is often survivable with slower queries), unchecked TOAST bloat can consume enormous disk space with minimal query-visible benefit, as the large values are accessed infrequently compared to TOAST pointer maintenance overhead.	\N	{postgresql}	\N	\N	\N
4642	PERF-SQL-VS-003-RC14	52	14	Mergeout memory constraints	If Tuple Mover resource pool MEMORYSIZE is too low, mergeout operations fail or are blocked waiting for memory, preventing container cleanup. **Architectural Context:** Vertica's hybrid architecture uses ROS (Read-Optimized Store) for disk-resident data and WOS (Write-Optimized Store) for in-memory temporary storage. The Tuple Mover background process continuously moves data from WOS to ROS (moveout) and merges multiple ROS containers into larger containers (mergeout). Mergeout is crucial because many small ROS containers degrade query performance—each container requires separate decompression, more file handles, and more intermediate merge operations in queries scanning the data. Vertica enforces a hard limit of 1024 ROS containers per projection per node to prevent metadata explosion and query fan-in explosion. When this limit is reached, Vertica refuses all new COPY/INSERT operations and returns ROS pushback errors. The strata algorithm groups containers by size and promotes them through exponential size tiers; containers must progress through strata, and mergeout operations are carefully scheduled to minimize re-touching of data. Long-running mergeouts on wide tables or insufficient mergeout parallelism can cause the pipeline to stall, allowing new containers from loads to accumulate faster than old containers are merged away.	\N	{vertica}	\N	\N	\N
6111	PERF-VEC-IDX-004-RC08	472	8	Skewed data undetected	Statistics assume uniform distribution but actual data is skewed, making cost estimates unreliable and leading to suboptimal query or search plans.	\N	{chromadb,milvus,qdrant,weaviate}	{"redis": "Single-threaded statistics collection; high query volume blocks statistics updates. Background optimization thread handles statistics incrementally", "milvus": "ANALYZE command not exposed; statistics maintained through segment optimization. Collection compaction updates statistics implicitly", "qdrant": "On-disk storage separates statistics from queries; can cause stale stats on frequently accessed collections. indexing_threshold changes affect statistics interpretation", "mongodb": "Statistics automatically collected; manual stats refresh available via compactCollection. Index statistics specific to search nodes; main node statistics different", "weaviate": "Statistics implicit in HNSW layer structure; no explicit statistics maintenance. Dynamic ef adjustment based on real-time performance implicitly handles stale stats", "postgresql": "ANALYZE command mandatory after index operations. autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor controls frequency. default_statistics_target (0-10000) controls sample size. Manual ANALYZE can be forced anytime without locking"}	\N	\N
4657	PERF-SQL-VS-004-RC15	53	15	Tuple Mover not completing moveout fast enough	Although moveout is separate from WOS capacity, if Tuple Mover is CPU-bound or I/O-bound and cannot complete moveout operations quickly, new loads cannot clear WOS space. **Architectural Context:** Vertica's WOS is an in-memory data store optimized for rapid ingestion of small, frequent loads (trickle loading). The WOS stores data unsorted and uncompressed, allowing writes without the I/O cost of disk access. Vertica automatically moves data from WOS to ROS either when a load completes or when WOS reaches capacity (whichever comes first in moveout), or when WOS becomes full (forced spillover). WOS capacity is fixed at 25% of available system RAM or 2 GB maximum, whichever is smaller. When WOS reaches capacity before moveout completes, subsequent data is spilled to disk (directly written to ROS), bypassing the in-memory fast path. This spillover degrades ingestion performance because: - Spilled data must be written to disk immediately (no memory buffering) - Spilled data is not pre-sorted, causing slower subsequent queries - ROS container count increases (each spill creates a small ROS container) - Query performance degrades due to more containers to scan and merge The WOS spill threshold is reached more frequently under high concurrency or large batch loads, especially if moveout cannot keep pace or if data is loaded with AUTO or DIRECT hints without proper configuration. Unlike queries on spilled data (which is still queryable), WOS spill primarily impacts *ingestion performance*, not query correctness, but it cascades into container count issues if frequent spills occur.	\N	{vertica}	\N	\N	\N
